2. Unsafe login program
This program directly joins user input into the SQL query.

import java.sql.*;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class UnsafeLogin {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(
            "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/b2_test",
            "root",
            "YOUR_PASSWORD"
        );

        System.out.print("Enter ID: ");
        String id = sc.nextLine();

        System.out.print("Enter Password: ");
        String pwd = sc.nextLine();

        String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id='"
                + id + "' AND pwd='" + pwd + "'";

        Statement st = con.createStatement();
        ResultSet rs = st.executeQuery(sql);

        if (rs.next()) {
            System.out.println("Login Successful");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid Login");
        }

        con.close();
        sc.close();
    }
}



3. Construct an adversarial input
Enter:
ID: admin
Password: ' OR '1'='1' --


The resulting SQL query is:
SELECT * FROM users
WHERE id='admin' AND pwd='' OR '1'='1' --'


Expected observation:
Unsafe login accepts the crafted input
Login Successful


The condition '1'='1' is always true. The comment marker -- ignores the remaining SQL text, allowing the query to return a record without the correct password.
B2.2 – Safe Login Using PreparedStatement
The following program uses placeholders (?) instead of joining user input directly into SQL.

import java.sql.*;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class SafeLogin {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(
            "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/b2_test",
            "root",
            "YOUR_PASSWORD"
        );

        System.out.print("Enter ID: ");
        String id = sc.nextLine();

        System.out.print("Enter Password: ");
        String pwd = sc.nextLine();

        String sql =
            "SELECT * FROM users WHERE id=? AND pwd=?";

        PreparedStatement ps = con.prepareStatement(sql);

        ps.setString(1, id);
        ps.setString(2, pwd);

        ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery();

        if (rs.next()) {
            System.out.println("Login Successful");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid Login");
        }

        con.close();
        sc.close();
    }
}



Enter the same adversarial input:
ID: admin
Password: ' OR '1'='1' -- 


PreparedStatement rejects the crafted input
Invalid Login


Reason: PreparedStatement treats the crafted password as ordinary text. It does not execute the input as an SQL condition.
Login test comparison
Test	Unsafe query	PreparedStatement
Correct ID and password	Login successful	Login successful
Wrong password	Login rejected	Login rejected
Crafted SQL input	May accept login	Rejects login

Note: This example uses plain-text passwords only to demonstrate SQL injection in a local assignment database. Real applications should store salted password hashes and verify them securely.
