B2.4 – Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
1. Escaping quote characters
One alternative is to replace single quotes in user input before creating the SQL query.

Example:
pwd = pwd.replace("'", "''");


This attempts to prevent a quote from ending the SQL string.

Limitation: Escaping rules depend on the database and SQL context. Incorrect or incomplete escaping can still leave security weaknesses.
2. Regular expression validation
Another alternative is to allow only selected characters:
if (!id.matches("[A-Za-z0-9]+") ||
    !pwd.matches("[A-Za-z0-9]+")) {

    System.out.println("Invalid Input");

    return;
}


This rejects characters outside the allowed pattern.

Limitation:
- It may reject legitimate passwords containing special characters.
- A new input format may require changes to the expression.
- It does not separate SQL commands from user input.
- Other SQL injection paths may remain if validation is incomplete.
3. Comparison
Method	Security	Limitation
Direct SQL concatenation	Unsafe	SQL injection possible
Quote escaping	Better, but weaker	Incorrect escaping may fail
Regular expression	Restricts input	May reject valid input
PreparedStatement	Recommended	Separates SQL structure from parameter values

4. Time Complexity Analysis
Let m represent the length of the input string.
Method	Time complexity
Quote escaping	O(m)
Regular expression validation	O(m) for a simple pattern
PreparedStatement parameter binding	O(m) to process the input values

All three methods also perform the database query. The actual database execution cost depends on table size and indexing.

PreparedStatement may reuse a prepared execution plan depending on the JDBC driver and database, but its main advantage is security rather than guaranteed faster execution.
